+Recent fuzzing-oriented approaches instead leverage large language models (LLMs) for end-to-end test-case transfer~\cite{fu2024sedar}. For example, \sedar{}~\cite{fu2024sedar} combines schema information and raw SQL text as input to an LLM to generate target-dialect seeds. However, such approaches still suffer from two fundamental limitations: 
 (i) First, due to limited understanding of target SQL dialects and weak structural awareness of multi-statement programs, LLM-based translation may introduce syntactic or semantic errors during generation, leading to invalid or inconsistent query behaviors.
 (ii) Second, to satisfy strict grammar constraints imposed by downstream fuzzers, parser-incompatible fragments are handled after translation rather than resolved during it. For example, \sedar{} temporarily comments them out during mutation and restores them before execution on the target DBMS, which retains the original query logic but leaves translation errors in these fragments unrepaired.
 
